{"name":"Glassine","hex":"#d7e2e5","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"glassine","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A light paper used as interleaving between artworks or stamp album pages.","Smooth, non-absorbing, grease- and moisture-resistant, supercalendered paper."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/glassine"}],"entries":[{"title":"Glassine","blocks":[{"text":"Glassine is a smooth and glossy paper that is air-, water-, and grease-resistant. It is typically available in densities between 50–90 g/m2 (0.010–0.018 lb/sq ft). It is naturally translucent, though dyes are sometimes added to the paper for color or opaqueness. Manufacturing takes place by first orienting the fibers in the same direction during the original pressing and drying followed immediately by supercalendering. Supercalendering passes the dried paper web through a stack of alternating steel-covered and fiber-covered rolls to increase density."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/44/1900_circa_Arthur_Weston_glassine_cover.jpg/330px-1900_circa_Arthur_Weston_glassine_cover.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Glassine","wikidata":"Q1515155"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b8/Pergamint%C3%BCte.jpg/500px-Pergamint%C3%BCte.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b8/Pergamint%C3%BCte.jpg/1280px-Pergamint%C3%BCte.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b8/Pergamint%C3%BCte.jpg/500px-Pergamint%C3%BCte.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Glassine envelope","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APergamint%C3%BCte.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb